er  and they are safe this is where people  are preparing hot meals and and serving  them as quickly as they can to people  that then queue up for another line and  then board these buses and we've seen  police officers and firefighters  effectively becoming porters really  helping people to load their bags carry  them onto the buses and  you know people are just carrying with  them  you know the precious little they have  left they've left their homes behind and  only brought what they can carry and  here they actually do  find a moment to sort of  take stock of what's happened before  they go on to other areas but here at  the medical border crossing things are  still quite chaotic and the volunteers  we've spoken to have said that  the polish government certainly is is is  stepping up but there's still need so  much more still needs to be done you  know we've seen  a million people cross over  in little more than a week ukraine is a  country of more than 40 million people  and if there is a big push if there is a  crush of people that has to run towards  the safety of poland in a short space of  time as the violence in ukraine  continues to get worse as russian  soldiers continue to push into  other cities  and push towards kiev then the crisis  here may outpace  the effort of the volunteers but also of  uh the governments and the different  ngos that are working here to try to get  people  the help that they need  yeah certainly i mean hundreds and  thousands of people have fled to poland  what's the general mood like where you  are what's the mood of  the volunteers and also the mood of  ukrainians is there is it one of  defiance is it one of anger is it one of  sadness how can you describe what people  are like there and what they're  experiencing from  an emotional perspective  absolutely that oscillates wildly  from day to day from moment to moment  uh we were interviewing a few people  that just in the middle of the interview  were overwhelmed and had to walk away  you know for the for the mothers the  children the elderly the vulnerable the  civilians crossing into poland is a  moment of of real relief um they may not  know where they're going to next but at  least they know they're safe on this  side of the border they're in eu  territory they're under the protection  of the polish border guard so there's a  great deal of of relief  many of the children aren't really old  enough to understand what's happening uh  but the ones that are old enough to  understand  um you know they have reef safety you  know there's a look of shock on a lot of  people's faces  and then when you come to the emotions  of people going the other way there's a  great deal of anger determination  and urgency  we've been standing here for a few hours  and we've seen at least three or four  different groups of men with heavy packs  medical gear  you know camouflage apparel we've  stopped to ask them where they're going  they're in generally quite a rush and  they say we're medics we're volunteer  fighters we need to know where the  border is we're trying to cross over to  the other side so you know it really  as much as there is uh relief and  anguish and and people who feel  desperate  the men going across to the other side  are are angry determined and  we more there's been more urgency in the  last 24 hours with the people we've  spoken to than we've seen in the last  few days so people are  are going over quicker they're going  over uh to try to get to the front lines  as quickly as possible  zane for people just joining us i just  want to let them know why we're crossing  to you now it's because we've obviously  just had a press conference from u.s  secretary of state anthony blinken and  the polish foreign minister in the south  east of the country where there are  hundreds of thousands of ukrainians  fleeing into poland i guess just for our  audience to recap can you just talk a  little bit about the significance of the  meeting between 